To determine if there is a cycle in the graph, we can try to find a path that starts and ends at the same node without repeating edges. Since the graph is relatively dense, it is highly likely to contain cycles.

We can use a graph traversal algorithm like Depth First Search (DFS) to detect cycles. During DFS, if we encounter a node that is currently in the recursion stack (i.e., it is an ancestor of the current node in the DFS tree), then a back edge exists, which indicates a cycle.

Let's start a DFS from node 0. We will keep track of visited nodes and the parent of each node in the DFS tree.

